# Where the API lives. Required — the app refuses to start without it rather than
# defaulting to an address no device can reach (localhost on a phone is the phone).
#
#   Android emulator   http://10.0.2.2:8080   (10.0.2.2 is the emulator's host loopback)
#   iOS simulator      http://localhost:8080
#   Physical device    http://<your machine's LAN IP>:8080
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://10.0.2.2:8080

# Where the sign-in link in the email points. The API appends
# `/auth/magic-link?token=…` to this, and the app answers that route either way,
# so moving from the custom scheme to a real domain is a change here plus the
# matching app-link association files — never a code change.
#
# Leave it unset and the app falls back to its own scheme from app.json ("chatroute",
# the first of the two it registers; "joychat" is kept so links from before the
# ChatRoute rename still open the app),
# which is what you want for a dev build. A custom scheme will not open from Expo
# Go, and several mail clients decline to linkify a non-HTTPS scheme, so expect to
# test the tap half on a dev build or by opening the URL directly.
#
#   Dev build          chatroute://              (link: chatroute:/auth/magic-link?token=…)
#   Production         https://app.example.com
EXPO_PUBLIC_MAGIC_LINK_ORIGIN=chatroute://

# Development builds only: the seed accounts the sign-in screen offers as chips.
# Entries are `role:email:password`, comma-separated; only the first two colons
# separate fields, so a password may contain a colon but not a comma, and
# surrounding whitespace is stripped from every field, so it cannot start or end
# with a space. One entry per role — a second for a role already listed is
# ignored, and an entry that could not sign anyone in is dropped rather than
# taking the sign-in screen down with it.
#
# Picking a chip fills the sign-in form. It does not set a role: signing in is the
# ordinary password path and the role you land in is whatever the server says that
# account holds, so a `role` here that disagrees with the server is a stale label,
# not a claim.
#
# Never set this for a build you ship. It is read behind `__DEV__`, which Metro
# eliminates from a release bundle along with the credentials inlined here.
#
# This is also the one variable kept out of `eas.json`'s `env` blocks on purpose.
# What is in them is there because a cloud build cannot read this file -- `.env*` is
# gitignored, and EAS falls back to `.gitignore` when there is no `.easignore`, so
# without them `EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL` is undefined and `lib/api-config.ts` throws at
# import, which is a crash on launch rather than a broken screen. Putting those
# in a committed file leaks nothing: an `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` value is inlined into the
# shipped bundle by definition. These are passwords, so they stay out, and `__DEV__`
# means a preview or production build has no use for them anyway.
#
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID` is in them too, since the iOS client exists.
# It has to be there rather than only here: `app.config.js` derives the iOS URL
# scheme from it at build time, so a cloud build without it has no Google sign-in
# on iOS. `EXPO_PUBLIC_MAGIC_LINK_ORIGIN` is absent because naming it would
# only restate the scheme: `readMagicLinkOrigin` falls back to `scheme` in
# `app.json`, and `chatroute://` and that fallback normalise to the same origin.
#
# `.github/workflows/build.yml` is a third source and reaches neither of these
# files: its iOS job bundles on the runner, where `.env` is not in the checkout and
# `eas.json` is never read, so it takes the same values from repository variables.
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_SEED_ACCOUNTS=customer:customer@example.com:pw,agent:agent@example.com:pw,admin:admin@example.com:pw
EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_SEED_ACCOUNTS=

# Google sign-in. Two OAuth clients from the Google Cloud console, and the
# arrangement between them is the fragile part of this feature — read this
# before "fixing" the web client out of a mobile app's configuration.
#
# The API verifies a Google ID token's audience against one client: the *web*
# one, because Better Auth registers the Google provider only when a client id
# and secret are configured, and a native client has no secret. Google's native
# sign-in library takes the web client alongside the platform one and mints the
# ID token with the web client as the audience — so the token a phone produces
# validates against the server's existing configuration, and mobile Google
# sign-in needs no API change. See
# docs/adr/ADR-0005-the-google-audience-arrangement.md.
#
# The web value is not a mobile-only setting to be invented: it is the same
# client the web client puts in NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID and the API puts in
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID. Copy it from either. If the three ever disagree, mobile is
# the one that is wrong.
#
# Leave either unset and the app still starts: the Google button is disabled and
# names the variable that is missing — the same call the web client makes, where
# the client id is optional and no Google section renders without it. Set one
# *wrong* and the app refuses to start, because a value that cannot be a client
# identifier has no honest reading.
#
#   Web       required on both platforms — this is the audience
#   iOS       required on iOS only; Android needs none, but it does need the
#             build's signing certificate SHA-1 registered against the Android
#             OAuth client (see README → Google sign-in)
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=
